93,90,16,18,24,24,92,1124,2533,1986,3363,582,1184,311,1477,17767,308,1124,8,1741,429,17767,220,16,21,15,77,481,220,16,18,24,24,74,284,220,16,1124,8,369,1045,7546,17767,595,1124,568,1096,374,264,13482,7767,5127,38357,23606,11,323,582,646,11625,432,1667,279,40565,21963,50078,40325,382,5338,11,582,3796,279,21963,50078,40325,311,1477,279,12196,4185,49109,320,91289,8,315,220,16,21,15,323,220,16,18,24,24,1447,16,13,17767,220,16,18,24,24,284,220,23,1124,50853,220,16,21,15,488,220,16,20,24,1124,340,17,13,17767,220,16,21,15,284,220,16,1124,50853,220,16,20,24,488,220,16,1124,340,18,13,17767,220,16,20,24,284,220,16,20,24,1124,50853,220,16,488,220,15,1124,3623,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nNotice that  \\[35\\cdot40=1400.\\]Find some integer $n$ with $0\\leq n<1399$ such that $n$ is the multiplicative inverse to 160 modulo 1399.\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"9e6a7463f88eb72894a2760eaeb44d8b301ca041d32a1ba70c4e17c53f2824cf"},"seconds":31.16201114654541,"seed":677984073878865790,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to solve the equation:\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nSince the gcd is 1, 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists. Now, we backtrack using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399.\n\nFrom the second step:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\]\nFrom the first step:\n\\[ 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\nSubstituting for 159 in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis equation shows that:\n\\[ 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is \\( n = 9 \\).\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{9}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to solve the equation:\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nSince the gcd is 1, 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists. Now, we backtrack using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399.\n\nFrom the second step:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\]\nFrom the first step:\n\\[ 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\nSubstituting for 159 in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis equation shows that:\n\\[ 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is \\( n = 9 \\).\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]","id":"number_theory:231"}
